  },
  "kvNotes": {
    "bf16": "The reference. Twice the cache of int8 for a perplexity difference too small to measure, and slower at depth. Useful for comparison, rarely the right choice.",
    "int8": "The quality default: +0.0009% perplexity is effectively free, and it holds its decode rate at depth better than anything except rk8v4.",
    "fp8": "No longer dominated on all three axes: it now has *better* perplexity than rk8v4, by 0.039%. That edge costs 26% more KV memory per token and 8.5% of decode speed at 32K depth -- a genuine trade, and a bad one for almost everyone, but a trade rather than a strict loss.",
    "rk8v4": "The best all-round choice. 23% smaller than int8, the flattest decode curve of any format measured, and +0.08% perplexity.",
    "rk4v4": "The context choice. 31% smaller than rk8v4 and within 3% of nvfp4, with better perplexity than nvfp4 (+0.21% against int8) and rk8v4's decode and prefill speed. Measured on a newer build than the other rows, so its speed and perplexity here are that session's ratios to rk8v4 and int8 applied to their rows, not a direct reading.",
    "k8v4": "Within 1.5% of rk8v4's size but the worst decode falloff measured (-25% by 32k) and slightly worse quality. Prefer rk8v4.",
    "nvfp4": "Maximum context: 45% smaller than int8. You pay about 15% of decode speed at a 32k cache and +0.36% perplexity for it."
  },
